{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>They shall not make a bald patch on their head.</b> Even though Israel too were warned concerning this, nevertheless both are necessary. For if Scripture had informed us only about Israel, I would have said that it is specifically the loss of an ordinary Israelite for his generation — which does not make such an impression — and therefore one need not grieve over him excessively to make a bald patch for him, because he likewise does not make so great a baldness in the world. But in the case of a priest who serves our Elohim and who has died — the loss is surely great for his generation, and he makes a baldness in the world; you would suppose that it is permitted for his kinsman the priest to make a bald patch over him — therefore Scripture informs us [that it is forbidden]. And if Scripture had informed us only about priests, I would have said that since the priest serves our Elohim, after his death he surely enters into the light of eternal life; therefore it is not fitting to grieve over his death, for it is for his own good. But in the case of an ordinary Israelite who is not assured that he will not see the face of Gehenna — you would suppose that it is permitted to grieve over him and to make a bald patch on his account — therefore Scripture informs us [that it is forbidden]." "<b>And he shall not profane his seed among his people.</b> It is wondrous in my eyes: if above they expounded 'among his people' to mean 'while the deceased is among his people' — what can be said of 'among his people' stated here? And its plain sense is 'among his people' — that he has disqualified his seed from being included among his people, that is, the priests, for he disqualified him from the laws of priesthood. But above it is impossible to explain thus, for if so, it would imply that he may not defile himself for a deceased priest who is not his flesh-and-blood kinsman — but in the case of an Israelite he may defile himself, and is this not a fortiori? And one cannot say that 'among his people' was employed for an inclusion, for if so, Scripture should have omitted it, since it was already stated ״כִּי אִם לִשְׁאֵרוֹ״ and so forth, and by this everything else is excluded. Therefore they needed to expound [it as] 'while the deceased is among his people.' But here it is according to its plain sense — from being included among his people."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:37:52.808415412Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}